# Exercise Steps

## Step 1: Prepare a Clean Repository State

### Verify the current branch is clean

```bash
git status
```

### Create and switch to a new branch named `GitWork`

```bash
git checkout -b GitWork
```

### Create `hello.xml` on the `GitWork` branch and commit it

```bash
echo "<data>Branch Data</data>" > hello.xml

git add hello.xml

git commit -m "Add hello.xml on GitWork branch"
```

### Switch back to the `master` branch

```bash
git checkout master
```

### Create the same file with different content and commit it

```bash
echo "<data>Master Data</data>" > hello.xml

git add hello.xml

git commit -m "Add hello.xml on master branch"
```

### View the commit graph

```bash
git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all
```

---

## Step 2: Resolve the Merge Conflict

### Merge `GitWork` into `master`

This command will trigger a merge conflict because both branches modified the same file differently.

```bash
git merge GitWork
```

### Check the repository status

```bash
git status
```

### View the conflicting changes

```bash
git diff
```

### Launch the P4Merge 3-way merge tool

```bash
git mergetool -t p4merge
```

### After resolving the conflict, commit the merge

```bash
git commit -m "Resolve merge conflict in hello.xml"
```

---

## Ignore Mergetool Backup Files

### Add `.orig` files to `.gitignore`

```bash
echo "*.orig" >> .gitignore
```

### Commit the updated `.gitignore`

```bash
git add .gitignore

git commit -m "Ignore mergetool backup files"
```

---

## Clean Up

### List all branches

```bash
git branch -a
```

### Delete the merged `GitWork` branch

```bash
git branch -d GitWork
```

### View the final commit history

```bash
git log --oneline --graph --decorate
```

